     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even though you've confirmed it. 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled daily dose of metformin, and I know of no indication that supports it. In a patient with normal kidney and liver function, the extra dose adds toxicity risk (lactic acidosis, severe GI effects) without more glucose lowering. Metformin's effect plateaus at about 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day.\n\nYou can still get the patient treated:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** I can enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or any other dose within the labeled range, right away.\n- **Get a second review if you think this dose is justified.** If there's a clinical reason I don't have, discuss it with pharmacy or the attending. The order can then be placed through a pathway that documents the override and the rationale.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
